egabytes you have per row this  is if you have growing data right every  day people are viewing more products so  we didn't have a static number of rows  so cache it save it to disk to memory  whatever figure out how big your data is  multiply the number of rows tone there's  megabytes per partition and divide by  the max megabytes you want to have per  partition so relatively straightforward  that's something you may not be aware of  as a new PI spark user if the defaults  have just worked well for you in the  past  all right another thing I didn't realize  is that good Python code can be really  hard to debug in PI spark so here you  can see the code from one of our  similarity jobs first we instantiate an  instance of that similarity job class we  generate the recs we call the right Rex  2d ease method and everything is done  that's the whole job and it's so  beautiful it's so elegant it's so simple  but if there's a problem you're gonna  have no idea what's going on there's a  ton of logic packed into each line of  code so if you run into errors like  super gnarly ones that you can't figure  out I would suggest that you unpack all  of your nice functions and classes and  run each line of code separately in a  notebook this kind of feel is  counterintuitive and it feels wrong  after you spent all the work trying to  to make everything nice but it will  actually save you time it along in the  long run and if something my boss always  tells me to do and I always resist and  then a week later I end up doing it  anyway so learn from my mistakes and  just do it right away and in particular  with PI spark you're gonna want to run  each step separately when you're  debugging and I'll go through this  example in a minute but the reason why  is because spark does something called  lazy evaluation so when you call it  transformation like multiplying two  columns or capitalizing a string column  or whatever it's not actually executed  until some action is called so one  common action is count like figuring how  big your data frame is another one is  take taking a row from the data frame  collect collecting stuff to data  whatever so if you set up a bunch of  transformations and don't call an action  spark tries to optimize everything so  that all the transformations are run in  the best way possible and sometimes this  works out really nicely and sometimes it  makes things really confusing so on the  left you can see that I'm taking some  data frame DF I turn it into a  coordinate matrix I turned that into a  row matrix and then I called column  similarities on that row matrix the red  indicates that there's an error so it  looks like there's an error during this  map tupple phase on the right you can  see a split all of the  steps out first I turn the data frame  into a coordinate matrix I take a row  from that and it works so that's an  option so things are executed I then  take that coordinate matrix and turn  into a row matrix try and take a row  from that and that's where the error is  actually taking place so if you don't  execute your transformations it'll it  can be very hard to figure out which one  is actually causing the issue in your  code and this is another thing that  feels kind of wrong like all you really  care about is the last step usually so  you're probably like well why do I need  to execute all these things I don't  really care about them because it will  save you time and money all right it'll  it'll just make your debugging a lot  easier if you can be sure that each line  of code is working the way that you  expect it to similarly I suggest that  you save intermediate steps to Amazon s3  or whatever other cheap storage space  you have HDFS I don't know Google  probably has something so writing the s3  is an action like count or take so that  will cause your transformations to be  executed and if you save your  intermediate steps you'll have them to  look back on later especially with super  long jobs that may 