irely get rid  of the connector as soon as possible so  now whenever my team runs PI spec code  we immediately write the results to s3  and they read them back in before doing  anything with them  all right at this point you may be  wondering what was causing that s3  expired token error and had I followed  the six suggestions I've given you so  far actually what a sidestep the heir  entirely I wouldn't have seen it at all  so I never would have figured out what's  going on on my own and actually we had  to work with data bricks to understand  what's happening and I didn't want to  use the same graphic so I flipped it I  thought that'd be different enough all  right so the secret to what happened is  buried in that gnarly stack trace and  showed you earlier I don't know if any  of you caught it maybe you're really  good at reading stack traces so here you  can see within the stack trace  there's something about snowflake when I  told you about the job earlier I  mentioned that the first thing we did  was read the data in from the database  and then all the calculations were in  spark this error was coming up to four  six hours into the job so snowflakes  should be gone by then so if we  shouldn't be doing anything all of the  errors should be involved with PI spark  and it turns out there's some issues  with the snowflake JDBC connector and  one way we can see this is by looking at  caching so first I'll show you how spark  caching is supposed to work here I have  some orders data that's been read in  from s3 on the bottom you can see I've  called cache on it and this command  takes no time at all  so with cash you're basically just  telling SPARC hey the next time you use  this data frame save it to disk or  memory so that it doesn't have to be  recalculated again so the next time an  action is called on it so it is a lazy  operation right nothing should be  happening at all and this is the way we  expect things to work  in contrast let's look at caching on  data t